                "name": "piwin_subagent_start",
                "arguments": {
                  "task": "Read-only task. File: experiment-data/negation-32k.txt (absolute path relative to workspace root).\n\nGOAL: Count how many of the 683 data-point sentences should be classified as label True.\n\nCONTEXT:\n- Lines 1-3 are instructions. Data starts at line 4.\n- Each data line format: Date: ... || User: ... || Instance: <CLAIM>\n- Each CLAIM is a single proposition about whether a word definition is appropriate/correct.\n- Label True means the CLAIM itself is a true statement; False means the CLAIM is a false statement.\n- Negation language matters: \"not\", \"never\", \"no X\", \"not a single\", \"in no context\", \"may not\", etc. reverse the truth of the underlying definition match.\n\nCLASSIFICATION RULES:\n1. Determine the intended WORD being defined and the DEFINITION given.\n2. Decide if the definition correctly matches the word (semantic/dictionary sense - common English WordNet-style definitions).\n3. Account for the claim's polarity:\n   - Affirmative claim + correct match → True\n   - Affirmative claim + incorrect match → False\n   - Negated claim + correct match → False (denies a true definition)\n   - Negated claim + incorrect match → True (correctly denies a wrong definition)\n4. Phrases like \"may be\", \"may commonly\", \"in certain contexts\", \"commonly\" are hedges but do NOT flip truth by themselves; only explicit negation flips.\n5. \"in no context X is appropriate definition of W\" = negation.\n6. \"X is an appropriate definition of W in no context\" = negation.\n7. \"Not a single W may be/is DEF\" = negation of W=DEF.\n8. \"No W is/may be DEF\" = negation.\n9. \"W may not refer/stand for DEF\" = negation.\n10. \"W does not/never/does never\" = negation.\n11. \"W is never DEF\" = negation.\n\nREQUIRED OUTPUT FORMAT (dense evidence report):\n- total_lines_data: N\n- true_count: N\n- false_count: N\n- true_count + false_count must equal 683\n- List EVERY line classified as True with: line_number | word | polarity(aff/neg) | match(correct/incorrect) | brief reason\n- Also provide a tally breakdown: correct_affirm, incorrect_affirm, correct_negated, incorrect_negated\n- Spot-check the first 20 and last 20 with explicit reasoning.\n\nDo NOT guess. Classify every single instance. Read the complete file. Return only the evidence report.",
                  "role": "scout",
                  "sessionName": "negation-true-count"
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "toolCall",
                "id": "call-50c6e204-7ba3-4bd2-adeb-942c3dfdf1c2-4|fc_1deacac4-ed4c-9445-a40f-f4d5afb3b384_1",
                "name": "bash",
                "arguments": {
                  "command": "# Extract just the Instance claims with line numbers for local analysis\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path('experiment-data/negation-32k.txt')\ntext = p.read_text()\nlines = text.splitlines()\nprint('total_lines', len(lines))\nprint('header:', lines[0][:80])\nclaims = []\nfor i, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\n    if '|| Instance: ' in line:\n        claim = line.split('|| Instance: ', 1)[1]\n        claims.append((i, claim))\nprint('n_claims', len(claims))\n# print first 5 and last 5\nfor i,c in claims[:5]:\n    print(f'{i}: {c[:100]}')\nprint('---')\nfor i,c in claims[-5:]:\n    print(f'{i}: {c[:100]}')\n# save claims to /tmp for processing if needed - wait, readonly - just print stats\nprint('chars', sum(len(c) for _,c in claims))\nPY"
